const title = "7 Best Heat Tape for Pipes in 2026: Self-Regulating vs Constant";
const description =
  "The best heat tape for pipes on Amazon from nVent Raychem, HeatTapePro, HEATIT, Wrap-On, Frost King and EasyHeat, compared on self-regulating vs constant wattage, thermostats, safety and install.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "raychem-frostguard-18",
    "name": "nVent Raychem FrostGuard Pipe Freeze Protection, 18 ft (FG1-18P)",
    "category": "Self-Regulating, 120V, Plug-In, Pipes up to 2.5 in, Overlap-Safe",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Self-regulating cable from nVent Raychem, a major heat-trace maker",
      "Listing says no overheating is possible, even when overlapped or closely spaced",
      "Works on metal and plastic pipes up to 2.5 inches in diameter",
      "Simple install: fix to the pipe, insulate, plug into a GFI-protected outlet",
      "No moving parts, so nothing to maintain, per the listing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The listing does not state watts per foot or the minimum temperature rating",
      "18 feet is a fixed length; measure the run before buying",
      "Usually costs more than constant-wattage tapes"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The safest, simplest plug-in cable for most homes: self-regulating, overlap-safe and made by the biggest name in heat trace.",
    "description": "Raychem is the name electricians associate with heat trace, and FrostGuard is nVent's plug-in version for homeowners. It is the pick for most people because it removes the biggest installation risk with heat tape: overheating. The cable is self-regulating, which means its heat output drops as the pipe warms and rises as it cools, and the listing says no overheating is possible even when the cable is overlapped or closely spaced. That makes it far more forgiving on valves, tees and awkward bends where a constant-wattage tape would have to be routed carefully so it never crosses itself. nVent lists it for metal and plastic pipes up to 2.5 inches in diameter, which covers ordinary supply lines in crawl spaces, garages and basements. The install steps are short: fix the cable to the pipe, cover it with pipe insulation, and plug it into a GFI-protected outlet. There are no moving parts and nothing to maintain. The trade-offs: the listing is light on numbers, with no watts per foot or minimum temperature stated, so check nVent's spec sheet for your climate; the 18-foot length is fixed, so measure the run before ordering, and nVent sells other lengths. It also costs more than basic tape. Buy the HEATIT 24-foot instead if budget matters most, or the Wrap-On for the lowest running cost."
  },
  {
    "id": "heattapepro-water-pipe-18",
    "name": "Radiant Solutions HeatTapePro Heat Tape for Water Pipe, 18 ft",
    "category": "Self-Regulating, Built-In Thermostat (On 37F, Off 50F), GlowCap, 10-Year Warranty",
    "badge": "Best Warranty",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Self-regulating cable with a thermostat that turns on at 37F and off at 50F",
      "GlowCap end cap shows the whole cable is working",
      "10-year warranty, per the listing",
      "For copper, steel, PVC, ABS, PEX and other water pipes",
      "Commercial-grade outer jacket and no heat-shrink connections, per the maker"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The listing says it is not for outdoor use",
      "Won't switch on above 37F, so test it with an ice pack first",
      "The 36-inch power cord is unheated and adds to the stated length"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "An indoor self-regulating pipe cable with a light that proves it works and the longest warranty here.",
    "description": "The most common complaint about heat tape is that you cannot tell whether it is working until a pipe freezes. HeatTapePro, from Minnesota-based Radiant Solutions, answers it with GlowCap, a lit end cap the maker says confirms the entire cable is powered. That is useful on a run hidden under insulation in a crawl space. The cable is self-regulating and has a built-in thermostat that switches it on at 37F and off at 50F, so it only draws power in cold weather. The listing approves it for every common water pipe material, including copper, steel, PVC, ABS and PEX, and says it has a commercial-grade outer jacket and no heat-shrink connections, which are a common failure point on cheaper cables. The 10-year warranty is the longest of any pipe cable in this guide. Read the listing's two cautions closely. First, this pipe model is not for outdoor use or for use inside pipes; Radiant Solutions points outdoor buyers to its separate Heat Tape Pro line. Second, because the thermostat stays off above 37F, the cable will not warm up on a mild day, so the maker tells you to test it with an ice pack before you install it. The 36-inch power cord is unheated. Buy the Raychem FrostGuard instead for a garage or crawl space with no outdoor exposure question, or the HEATIT in-line cable for buried lines."
  },
  {
    "id": "heatit-24ft-3w",
    "name": "HEATIT 24 ft Self-Regulating Pipe Heat Cable with Thermostat, 3W/ft",
    "category": "Self-Regulating, 3 W/ft, 120V, Thermostat (On 38F, Off 50F), ETL Certified",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Self-regulating at 3 watts per foot, a low running cost",
      "Thermostat activates at 38F and switches off at 50F",
      "Whole cable ETL certified and plug UL certified, per the listing",
      "Rated to keep water flowing down to -40F",
      "Sold in lengths from 2 feet to 160 feet"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Smaller brand than Raychem or EasyHeat",
      "Only a 3-foot power cord, so plan where the outlet is",
      "Listing marketing is heavy; check the manual for insulation rules"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A low-cost self-regulating cable with full ETL certification and lengths for almost any run.",
    "description": "If you want self-regulating cable without paying Raychem prices, the HEATIT 3W/ft cable is the value choice. It uses the same basic technology: the heating core adjusts its output to the surrounding temperature, and the maker says it never overheats. A built-in thermostat activates it at 38F and turns it off at 50F, so in a heated basement it sits idle most of the winter. The listing rates it to keep water flowing down to -40F at 3 watts per foot, which is less than half the 7 watts per foot of older constant-wattage tapes and keeps running costs low across a long run. Certification is the detail to check on any budget heat cable, and HEATIT's listing says the whole cable is ETL certified, not only the plug, and that the plug is UL certified. ETL is a recognized North American safety mark, the same role UL plays. It is made for metal and plastic pipes, including copper, PVC and stainless steel, and HEATIT sells it in lengths from 2 feet to 160 feet, so you can buy a length close to your run. The trade-offs: HEATIT is a smaller brand than Raychem, the power cord is only 3 feet long, and the listing leans on marketing, so read the manual for its rules on insulation and spacing. Buy the Raychem FrostGuard instead if you want the established name."
  },
  {
    "id": "wrap-on-31015",
    "name": "Wrap-On Pipe Heating Cable, 15 ft, 120V, Built-In Thermostat (31015)",
    "category": "2 W/ft, Built-In Thermostat (On 38F), Press-to-Test Button, Made in USA",
    "badge": "Lowest Wattage",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Just 2 watts per foot, which Wrap-On says uses up to 65% less energy than 7 W/ft cables",
      "Built-in thermostat turns the cable on at 38F",
      "Press-to-test button checks the cable before or after installing",
      "Rated to prevent frozen pipes down to -50F with insulation",
      "Made in the USA"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The listing does not say it is self-regulating, so follow the spacing rules",
      "Must be insulated, and insulation is sold separately",
      "For rigid pipes only: PVC, CPVC, copper and metal"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most energy-frugal pipe cable here, with a test button, for rigid pipes you will insulate.",
    "description": "Wrap-On has made pipe heating cable in the USA for decades, and the 31015 is a 15-foot plug-in cable built around low wattage. At 2 watts per foot, Wrap-On says it uses up to 65% less energy than cables that run at 7 watts per foot. That matters because heat tape can run for weeks at a time in a cold snap. The built-in thermostat turns the cable on at 38F, and the listing rates it to prevent frozen pipes down to -50F, with one condition: you must wrap it, insulate it, and plug it in. The low output relies on insulation to hold the heat against the pipe, and the insulation is sold separately. The standout feature is the press-to-test button, which lets you check that the cable heats before you bury it under insulation and again each fall. Wrap-On lists it for rigid pipes: PVC, CPVC, copper and metal. The trade-offs are worth knowing. The listing does not describe the cable as self-regulating, so treat it as a fixed-output cable and follow the instructions on spacing and whether it may cross itself. It is not listed for flexible PEX, and an uninsulated run will not reach the rated protection. Buy the Raychem FrostGuard or HeatTapePro instead for PEX or for valves where the cable must overlap."
  },
  {
    "id": "frost-king-hc12a",
    "name": "Frost King HC12A Automatic Electric Heat Cable Kit, 12 ft",
    "category": "Constant Wattage, 7 W/ft, 120V, Built-In Thermostat, Metal and Plastic Pipes",
    "badge": "Best Budget Kit",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Widely sold, inexpensive pre-assembled kit",
      "Built-in thermostat switches it on automatically",
      "Rated to keep water flowing down to -40F",
      "For metal and plastic water pipes",
      "Sold in 3, 6, 9, 12 and 24-foot versions"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Constant-wattage: never overlap or cross the cable",
      "7 W/ft uses more power than self-regulating or low-wattage cables",
      "Thin listing: check the instructions for pipe and insulation limits"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The classic hardware-store heat tape kit: cheap and automatic, if you install it carefully.",
    "description": "Frost King is the brand most people picture when they think of heat tape, and the HC12A is its classic pre-assembled kit for a 12-foot stretch of pipe. It is a constant-wattage cable with a built-in thermostat, so it switches on by itself as the pipe gets cold. The listing gives 120 volts, 7 watts per foot and protection for metal and plastic water pipes down to -40F. At 7 watts per foot, the 12-foot cable draws about 84 watts when it is on, which is modest for one pipe but adds up across a long winter. Frost King also sells it in 3, 6, 9 and 24-foot lengths, so you can match the run. The trade-off is the reason this is not the top pick. A constant-wattage cable puts out the same heat everywhere along its length, so if it overlaps or crosses itself, the doubled section can overheat. You must run it straight or spiral it with gaps, exactly as the instructions show, and never let it touch itself. The listing is thin: it does not explain insulation rules, pipe material limits for plastic, or the thermostat set point beyond the 40F in the title, so read the printed instructions before you install. Buy a self-regulating cable such as the HEATIT or Raychem instead for valves, tees and crowded runs."
  },
  {
    "id": "easyheat-ahb-016",
    "name": "EasyHeat AHB-016 Cold Weather Valve and Pipe Heating Cable, 6 ft",
    "category": "Pre-Assembled, Grounded Plug, Built-In Thermostat, Short Runs and Valves",
    "badge": "Best for Short Runs",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Short 6-foot length suits a single valve, meter or short pipe",
      "Grounded plug and built-in thermostat, per the listing",
      "Pre-assembled with instructions",
      "From EasyHeat, a long-established heat cable maker",
      "Sold in other lengths for longer runs"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The listing does not state wattage or temperature ratings",
      "Listed as made in China",
      "Too short for most crawl space runs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A small plug-in cable for one exposed valve or a short pipe, from a well-known maker.",
    "description": "Not every frozen-pipe problem is a long run. Often it is one valve at the water meter, a short pipe through an exterior wall, or the supply line to a washing machine in a cold garage. The EasyHeat AHB-016 is a 6-foot cable built for exactly that job, and the listing names it a cold weather valve and pipe heating cable. EasyHeat is one of the longest-running names in pipe heating cable, and this kit is pre-assembled, with a grounded plug, a built-in thermostat and instructions included, so there is no wiring to do. Six feet of cable is enough to wrap a valve body and run along a short length of pipe either side of it, the places where water tends to freeze first. The trade-off is information. The Amazon listing is thin: it does not state watts per foot, the thermostat's switch-on temperature or the minimum temperature it protects to, and it lists the cable as made in China. Before you buy, look up the AHB series spec sheet and instructions from EasyHeat, especially the rules on whether the cable may overlap itself on a valve, because many fixed-output cables may not. Buy a longer self-regulating cable, such as the HEATIT 24-foot or Raychem FrostGuard, if the pipe run is longer than a few feet or the valve needs the cable doubled over."
  },
  {
    "id": "heatit-inline-20ft",
    "name": "HEATIT Self-Regulating In-Line Pipe Heat Cable, 20 ft",
    "category": "In-Pipe, Self-Regulating, CSA NSF61, Fluoropolymer Jacket, Built-In GFCI Plug",
    "badge": "Best for Buried Lines",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Installs inside the pipe, for buried or enclosed lines you cannot reach",
      "CSA NSF61 certified for drinking water, per the listing",
      "Built-in GFCI plug",
      "Self-regulating, with a tough fluoropolymer jacket",
      "For potable water, wastewater and lines fed from lakes or springs"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "In-pipe install needs fittings and more plumbing work",
      "Reduces flow area inside small pipes",
      "Listing claims to be the only fully certified product; treat that as marketing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A self-regulating cable that goes inside the pipe, for buried and hidden lines no wrap-on tape can reach.",
    "description": "Every other cable in this guide wraps around the outside of a pipe, which is useless when the pipe is buried under a driveway, runs through a wall you cannot open, or feeds a cabin from a lake or spring. The HEATIT in-line cable is the answer for those jobs: it is designed to be installed inside the pipe, in direct contact with the water. Because of that, certification for drinking water matters, and HEATIT's listing says the whole cable is CSA certified to NSF61, the standard for products that touch potable water. It is self-regulating, so output adjusts to temperature without overheating, and it has a fluoropolymer outer jacket for long life in water. The plug has a built-in GFCI, which you want on any cable sitting in water. The listing names homes, barns and vacation properties as uses, and says it can run continuously or as a precaution. The trade-offs are real. Getting a cable into a pipe means fitting a tee or an entry fitting, which is plumbing work, and the cable takes up some of the pipe's inside diameter, which matters on small lines. The listing's claim that it is the only fully certified product on the market is marketing, not something we can confirm. Buy an external cable, such as the Raychem FrostGuard, for any pipe you can reach."
  }
