        <div id="what-is-heat-tape" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Is Heat Tape for Pipes? A Heated Cable That Stops Freezing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat tape is a flat electric heating cable that you attach along a water pipe to keep it above freezing. The name is a holdover from older products that really were tape-like; most of what you buy today is a cable with a plug on one end. Plumbers and electricians call the same thing heat trace or heat tracing, and you will see pipe heating cable, heat cable and pipe heat tape used for the same products.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is a targeted fix. Heat tape does not warm a room; it puts a small amount of heat exactly where a pipe loses it, on lines that run through crawl spaces, unheated garages, exterior walls, mobile home skirting and well houses. Paired with insulation, a few watts per foot is enough to keep water moving through a hard freeze.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat Tape vs Roof and Gutter De-Icing Cable</h3>

          <p>
            Roof and gutter de-icing cable looks similar and shows up in the same search results, but it is a different product with different ratings, clips and installation rules, made to melt channels through ice dams on shingles and in downspouts. This guide covers pipe cables only. Do not use a roof cable on a water pipe, or a pipe cable on a roof, unless the maker's listing approves both uses; some cables in search results do, but the picks below were chosen for pipes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Heat Tape Belongs</h3>

          <p>
            Use it on supply lines in crawl spaces and under mobile homes, pipes in unheated garages and attics, lines along exterior walls, water meters and valves, well pump lines and RV hookups. It is not a substitute for draining and shutting off a hose bib, which is a separate job covered in our guide to <a href="/how-to-winterize-outdoor-faucets/">winterizing outdoor faucets</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Self-Regulating vs Constant-Wattage Heat Tape: Which Kind Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pipe heat cable falls into one of two families, and the difference decides how you can install it and how safe it is when something goes wrong.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Self-Regulating Heat Tape</h3>

          <p>
            Self-regulating cable has a conductive polymer core between two bus wires. As the core cools, it conducts more and heats more; as it warms, its resistance rises and output falls. Each inch of cable responds to its own temperature, so a section against a cold valve works harder than a section in still air. Raychem's FrostGuard listing says the cable cannot overheat even when overlapped, which is why self-regulating cable is the easier and safer choice for valves, tees and crowded runs. It costs more up front.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Constant-Wattage Heat Tape</h3>

          <p>
            Constant-wattage cable, like the Frost King HC12A, has a resistance wire that puts out the same watts per foot everywhere along its length whenever it is on. A thermostat switches it on and off, but it cannot turn itself down. That makes it cheaper, but it must never overlap or cross itself, because the doubled section can overheat. Install it exactly as the instructions show.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Low-Wattage Fixed-Output Cable</h3>

          <p>
            Some cables, like the Wrap-On 31015, run at a low fixed output of around 2 watts per foot and rely on insulation to hold the heat in. They are cheap to run, but treat them as fixed-output cable and follow the maker's spacing and overlap rules.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            Pick self-regulating for valves, PEX, plastic pipe, long runs and any place you cannot inspect. Pick constant wattage for a short, straight, easy-to-check pipe on a tight budget. Pick in-pipe cable only when the outside of the pipe is unreachable.
          </p>

