        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Heat Tape Safety: The Four Rules That Prevent Fires
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat tape is safe when it is installed the way the maker intends and dangerous when it is not. Old, damaged and improperly installed pipe heating cables have been linked to house fires, which is why these rules are not optional.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rule 1: Plug Into a GFCI Outlet</h3>

          <p>
            Use a ground-fault (GFCI) protected outlet for every heat cable. Pipes, water and cold, damp spaces are where a damaged cable is most likely to leak current, and a GFCI cuts power in a fraction of a second. Raychem's listing names a GFI-protected supply as part of the install, and the HEATIT in-line cable has GFCI built into its plug. Do not run heat tape from a long indoor extension cord.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rule 2: Never Overlap or Cross Constant-Wattage Tape</h3>

          <p>
            A constant-wattage cable that crosses itself doubles its heat at that spot. Run it straight along the pipe or spiral it with even gaps, as the instructions show. Self-regulating cable is designed to tolerate overlap; check the listing before assuming yours is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rule 3: Insulate Over It Only if the Maker Allows</h3>

          <p>
            Many cables are designed to be covered with insulation, and some, like the Wrap-On, require it. Others restrict the type and thickness. Use only the insulation the instructions specify, usually fiberglass or a foam rated for the cable, and never cover the thermostat if the instructions say to leave it exposed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rule 4: Follow the Instructions and Local Code</h3>

          <p>
            Follow the maker's instructions for pipe material, length, mounting tape and inspection, and follow your local electrical code. Hardwired heat trace and anything in a commercial building should be installed by a licensed electrician. If a cable is cracked, discolored or brittle, replace it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Install Heat Tape on Water Pipes, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most plug-in pipe cables install in under an hour. The steps below are the common pattern; your cable's instructions take priority wherever they differ.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Measure the Pipe and Check the Cable</h3>

          <p>
            Measure the full length of pipe you need to protect, add extra for each valve, and buy a cable at least that long. Before installing, plug it in and confirm it heats. Cables with a thermostat may need an ice pack on the sensor, as the HeatTapePro listing advises, and the Wrap-On has a press-to-test button.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Attach the Cable to the Pipe</h3>

          <p>
            Run the cable along the underside of the pipe, where water freezes first, or spiral it if the instructions allow. Fasten it every foot or so with the tape the maker specifies, which is often glass or aluminum tape rather than ordinary electrical tape. On plastic pipe, many instructions call for aluminum tape over the pipe to help spread heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Place the Thermostat Correctly</h3>

          <p>
            The built-in thermostat must sense the coldest pipe temperature. Most instructions say to tape it against the pipe, not dangle it in the air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Insulate and Plug In</h3>

          <p>
            If the instructions allow, cover the pipe and cable with insulation and seal the seams. Plug into a GFCI outlet and check the indicator light, if your cable has one, each fall before the first freeze.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-warm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Warm Does Heat Tape Get? Warm, Not Hot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pipe heat tape is designed to keep water just above freezing, not to heat it. On a self-regulating cable, the surface temperature rises only until the output balances the heat lost to the pipe and air, and the output falls as the pipe warms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thermostat Set Points</h3>

          <p>
            The thermostats in these cables switch on around 37F to 40F and off around 50F, per the listings: HeatTapePro on at 37F and off at 50F, HEATIT on at 38F and off at 50F, Wrap-On on at 38F, and Frost King's title gives 40F. Above those temperatures the cable sits idle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watts Per Foot</h3>

          <p>
            Pipe cables in this guide range from 2 watts per foot for the Wrap-On to 7 watts per foot for the Frost King. Higher wattage delivers more heat in severe cold but runs hotter and uses more power, which is one reason the insulation and overlap rules matter more on constant-wattage cable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much Electricity Does Heat Tape Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cost is simple arithmetic: watts per foot times length gives the watts drawn while the cable is on. A 12-foot cable at 7 watts per foot draws about 84 watts; a 15-foot cable at 2 watts per foot draws about 30.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Thermostats Cut the Bill</h3>

          <p>
            A cable without a thermostat runs whenever it is plugged in, all winter. A built-in thermostat that switches on below 37F to 40F means the cable only runs on cold days, and a self-regulating cable draws less still when the pipe is not very cold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Insulation Saves More Than Wattage</h3>

          <p>
            Insulating the pipe over the cable, where allowed, is the cheapest way to cut running cost. It holds heat at the pipe so the cable runs less, and on low-wattage cables it is required for the rated protection.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Long Does Heat Tape Last? Inspect It Every Fall
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no single lifespan for heat tape. It depends on the cable type, sunlight, physical damage and how many winters it has cycled. Radiant Solutions backs its HeatTapePro pipe cable with a 10-year warranty, which is a useful marker of what the maker expects.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs It Needs Replacing</h3>

          <p>
            Replace any cable with cracked or brittle jacket, discolored or scorched spots, exposed wires, a damaged plug, or a thermostat that no longer clicks on in the cold. Do not tape over damage and keep using it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Fall Checklist</h3>

          <p>
            Each fall, unplug the cable, check its full length and the insulation over it, test it with ice or the test button, and confirm the GFCI outlet trips and resets. If the cable has an indicator light, check it once the weather turns cold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pipe-materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Can You Use Heat Tape on PVC and PEX Pipe?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, if the cable is approved for it. Plastic pipe is where installation rules matter most, because plastic softens with heat and does not spread heat along its length as well as copper.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">PVC and CPVC</h3>

          <p>
            Wrap-On lists PVC and CPVC among the rigid pipes its 31015 suits, and Frost King, HEATIT and Raychem list plastic pipes generally. Many instructions call for aluminum tape over plastic pipe under the cable to spread heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">PEX</h3>

          <p>
            Radiant Solutions names PEX in its HeatTapePro listing. A self-regulating cable is the better match for PEX and other flexible plastic, because it limits its own output. Check your cable's listing for PEX before buying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Copper and Steel</h3>

          <p>
            Metal pipe is the easiest case. Every cable here lists metal pipes, and copper spreads heat well along the run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stop-freezing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Stop Pipes Freezing: Heat Tape Is One Layer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat tape works best as part of a plan. These steps reduce the chance of a burst pipe whether or not you install a cable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Seal and Insulate First</h3>

          <p>
            Seal drafts around crawl space vents, rim joists and pipe penetrations, and insulate exposed pipes. Cold air moving across a pipe freezes it far faster than still air. For garages, a <a href="/best-garage-door-insulation-kit/">garage door insulation kit</a> keeps pipes along the walls warmer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Know Your Water Heater and Main Shutoff</h3>

          <p>
            Pipes near a water heater in an unheated space are a common trouble spot. If you are replacing one in a garage or basement, our guides to the <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> and <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">best tankless water heaters</a> cover freeze protection and placement, since tankless units have their own freeze rules. Know where your main shutoff is before the first cold snap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drip Faucets and Open Cabinets in a Hard Freeze</h3>

          <p>
            In an extreme cold snap, a slow drip from a faucet on an exterior wall keeps water moving, and opening sink cabinet doors lets room heat reach the pipes. These are emergency measures, not a substitute for insulation or heat tape on a problem pipe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mobile-homes-rvs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Heat Tape for Mobile Homes, RVs and Well Lines
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mobile homes, RVs and wells are where heat tape is most common, because the water line runs exposed to outside air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mobile Homes</h3>

          <p>
            The supply line usually rises from the ground into the floor under the skirting. Wrap the riser and the exposed line with a self-regulating cable, insulate it, and plug it into a GFCI-protected outlet near the riser. Check the skirting for gaps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">RVs</h3>

          <p>
            For a winter campsite, run a heated hose or a self-regulating cable along the fresh water hose and protect the campground spigot. Choose a cable whose listing allows outdoor use; the HeatTapePro pipe model is not rated outdoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Well Lines and Buried Pipes</h3>

          <p>
            Well house lines and pressure tanks can be protected with an external cable. For a line that is buried too shallow or enclosed where you cannot reach it, an in-pipe cable like the HEATIT in-line model is the option, installed through a fitting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What Length of Heat Tape Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy by the length of pipe you need to protect, plus extra for valves. A cable that is too short leaves the end of the run unprotected, and most plug-in cables cannot be cut.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Straight Runs</h3>

          <p>
            For a straight run, the cable length roughly equals the pipe length. Choose the next size up if your measurement falls between sizes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Valves and Spiral Wrapping</h3>

          <p>
            Each valve or meter needs extra cable to wrap around the body, and spiral wrapping uses more cable per foot of pipe. Check the instructions for the allowance. On constant-wattage cable, spacing the spiral evenly so it never touches itself matters more than coverage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Long Runs and Bulk Cable</h3>

          <p>
            For runs over about 100 feet, or pipes larger than the plug-in kits cover, bulk self-regulating cable cut to length with a separate power connection kit is the professional route. That usually means hardwiring or a GFCI connection kit, and an electrician.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlapping Constant-Wattage Tape</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A constant-wattage cable that crosses itself doubles its heat at that spot and can overheat. Run it straight or spiral it with even gaps, or buy a self-regulating cable for valves and bends.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging Into an Outlet Without GFCI</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A damaged cable near water can leak current. Always use a GFCI-protected outlet, test it each fall, and do not run heat tape from a long indoor extension cord.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Insulating Over a Cable That Does Not Allow It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some cables require insulation, others restrict it. Use only the insulation type and thickness the instructions name, and leave the thermostat where the maker says.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Thermostat Dangling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thermostat hanging in warmer air may never switch the cable on. Tape it against the coldest part of the pipe, as the instructions show.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Testing or Inspecting the Cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heat tape sits unused for most of the year. Test it each fall with ice or the test button, and replace any cable with a cracked jacket, scorch marks or a damaged plug.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

