        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best heat tape for pipes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the nVent Raychem FrostGuard is the best heat tape for pipes. It is self-regulating, its listing says it cannot overheat even when overlapped, and it suits metal and plastic pipes up to 2.5 inches. Choose the HEATIT 24-foot for value, the Wrap-On for the lowest wattage, and the HEATIT in-line cable for buried lines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is heat tape?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Heat tape is an electric heating cable that runs along a water pipe to keep it above freezing. It plugs into an outlet, usually has a built-in thermostat that switches it on near 38F to 40F, and works best under insulation. It is also called heat trace, heat cable or pipe heating cable. Roof de-icing cable is a different product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is self-regulating heat tape better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, yes. Self-regulating cable lowers its own output as the pipe warms, so it uses less power and is far more tolerant of overlaps on valves and bends. Raychem's listing says FrostGuard cannot overheat even when overlapped. Constant-wattage tape is cheaper, but it must never cross itself and needs more careful installation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How warm does heat tape get?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pipe heat tape keeps water just above freezing rather than making it hot. The thermostats on the cables in this guide switch on at about 37F to 40F and off around 50F, per their listings. Output ranges from 2 watts per foot on the Wrap-On to 7 watts per foot on the Frost King. Self-regulating cable reduces output as it warms.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does heat tape last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies with the cable type, sunlight, damage and the number of winters it has cycled. Radiant Solutions offers a 10-year warranty on its HeatTapePro pipe cable. Inspect every cable each fall and replace any with a cracked jacket, scorch marks, a damaged plug or a thermostat that no longer turns on in the cold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put insulation over heat tape?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, but only if the maker allows it and only with the type of insulation the instructions name. Some cables, like the Wrap-On, require insulation to reach their rated protection. Others limit the thickness or type, or tell you to leave the thermostat exposed. Read the instructions first; covering a cable the wrong way is a known fire risk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does heat tape need a GFCI outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Plug every heat cable into a GFCI-protected outlet. Pipes and damp crawl spaces are where a damaged cable is most likely to leak current, and a GFCI cuts power almost instantly. Raychem's install steps call for a GFI-protected supply, and the HEATIT in-line cable has GFCI built into its plug. Avoid long indoor extension cords.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

